\begin_inset Formula $\overrightarrow{v}$
\end_inset

 as the hyportenuse.
 It should be noted however that this time v',
 vo and v vectors are different from Case I while the magnitudes of v' and vo are the same.
 Our problem Case II is independent in this regard from case I and we can choose the same names without loss of generality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Proceeding to solve the question
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\dfrac{AB}{v'}=\dfrac{d_{w}}{v_{o}}$
\end_inset

 (By similarity of triangles,
 from figure)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Rightarrow d_{w}=\dfrac{v_{o}}{v'}.AB$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Time 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}$
\end_inset

 to cross the river and reach the point (say C ) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}=\dfrac{AB}{v'}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Time 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{2}$
\end_inset

 to cross the distance dw back to B
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $t_{2}=\dfrac{d_{w}}{u}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Total time = 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}+t_{2}=\dfrac{AB}{v'}+\dfrac{d_{w}}{u}=\dfrac{AB}{v'}+\dfrac{\dfrac{v_{o}}{v'}.AB}{u}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Proceeding to solve the problem by comparing both the cases,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\dfrac{AB}{\sqrt{v'^{2}-v_{o}^{2}}}=\dfrac{AB}{v'}+\dfrac{\dfrac{v_{o}}{v'}.AB}{u}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Solving further,
 we get the required form,
 first cancelling AB and cross multiplying v'
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\dfrac{v'}{\sqrt{v'^{2}-v_{o}^{2}}}-1=\dfrac{v_{o}}{u}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Rightarrow u=\dfrac{v_{o}}{\dfrac{v'}{\sqrt{v'^{2}-v_{o}^{2}}}-1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Calculating the value,
 u = 3 km/hr
\end_layout

